-- Newsroom drafts for 2026-11-10 (Tuesday). Loaded once into EDITORIAL_REVIEW; an editor approves before anything is published.
-- Facts restated in Boston Daily's own words with attribution. Every story carries its own photo; none was used in an earlier package or in the open 2026-11-09 draft (grepped content/ and seed/).
-- Wikimedia Commons and loc.gov were unreachable from the newsroom sandbox on this run. Photo URLs are original-file Commons URLs built from file names found in web search (MD5 path); all are U.S. government or pre-1927 Library of Congress/archive photos. Editor: confirm each photo loads and matches its caption; the image check removes any that do not.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261110-hegseth-impeachment-vote', 'massie-forces-house-vote-hegseth-impeachment-eight-articles-iran-war-vote-by-wednesday', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'congress', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Massie files eight impeachment articles against Hegseth, setting up a House vote that could come by Wednesday, Nov. 11', 'The Kentucky Republican used a privileged resolution on Monday, Nov. 9, which gives the House two legislative days to act. GOP leaders could still table it, and some Republicans privately say it could pass.', 'Rep. Thomas Massie, R-Ky., introduced eight articles of impeachment against Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth on the House floor on Monday, Nov. 9, forcing the lame-duck House to deal with the measure within two legislative days. That could put a vote on the floor by Wednesday, Nov. 11, Fox News and WPDE reported.

Massie read the resolution aloud for about an hour and 20 minutes, The Week reported. He first tried the move in September, but the effort lapsed when House leaders ended the session early.

## What the articles charge

Three of the articles concern the Iran war, including claims that Hegseth waged it without authorization from Congress and kept it going past the 60-day limit in the 1973 War Powers Resolution, according to The Week. The others cover lethal strikes on suspected drug boats, the capture of Venezuela''s Nicolás Maduro, combat operations in Yemen, a failure to protect civilians, and alleged retaliation against Sen. Mark Kelly. "We''re not indicting him for being bad at his job," Massie said, as quoted by WPDE.

## The vote math

Republicans hold 218 seats to the Democrats'' 214, so as few as three GOP defections or absences could carry the measure, The Week reported. Several House Republicans told Fox News Digital that Hegseth "could very well be impeached," citing absences by members who lost their races. Most Republicans are still expected to oppose it, and leaders can move to table the resolution or send it to committee, either of which would effectively end it.

## Reactions

Speaker Mike Johnson called impeaching a Cabinet secretary during active hostilities a "terrible, terrible look," WPDE reported. Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries, D-N.Y., said he is a "hard yes." Hegseth called Massie a "disgruntled, defeated congressman" and said he would hold a news conference on Tuesday, Nov. 10. President Trump has said Hegseth is "doing a very good job," and The Week reported that Trump said he would stand by Hegseth even if the House impeaches him.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Flickr_-_USCapitol_-_Rayburn_House_Office_Building_(1).jpg', 'The Rayburn House Office Building on Capitol Hill, Washington (file). Photo: Architect of the Capitol via Wikimedia Commons, public domain', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-10.sql', 9, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-10T10:45:00.000Z', '2026-11-10T10:45:00.000Z');
