INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261110-scotus-big-bend-wall', 'supreme-court-lifts-order-big-bend-border-wall-construction-resumes-jackson-dissents', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'courts', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Supreme Court lets border wall work resume in Texas''s Big Bend region, with only Justice Jackson objecting', 'The unsigned order on Monday, Nov. 9, pauses a federal judge''s injunction while the case goes on. The administration has also asked the justices to take up the dispute.', 'The Supreme Court on Monday, Nov. 9, allowed the Trump administration to resume building border wall and related infrastructure in the Big Bend region of West Texas, SCOTUSblog and the Texas Tribune reported. The unsigned order puts on hold an injunction issued in October by U.S. District Judge Kathleen Cardone in El Paso. Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson said she would have denied the request and gave no explanation.

## The case

Cardone had barred the Department of Homeland Security from continuing construction until it complied with environmental and other laws. She found the challengers were likely to show that the Homeland Security secretary lacked authority to issue three waivers of those laws, SCOTUSblog reported. The plaintiffs are an environmental group, a river guide who owns land in the area and a group formed to preserve a church.

The justices wrote that the government''s interest "outweighs any aesthetic, recreational, and environmental harm" the challengers alleged, the Texas Tribune reported. The court also cited government costs of nearly half a million dollars a day while work was on hold, according to SCOTUSblog.

## The project

The Big Bend sector covers more than 500 miles of the border, including nearly 200 miles of the Rio Grande Wild and Scenic River corridor, CBS News reported. The plan calls for a 30-foot barrier, more than 200 miles of patrol roads and about 17 miles of vehicle barriers, according to Customs and Border Protection figures cited by CBS. The challengers say Big Bend accounts for only 0.3% to 2.2% of border-wide apprehensions.

The order is temporary. It lasts until the Supreme Court decides whether to hear the case or the dispute is resolved in the lower court, the Texas Tribune reported.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/TEXAS-BIG_BEND_NATIONAL_PARK_-_NARA_-_545899.jpg', 'Big Bend National Park, Texas (file). Photo: U.S. National Archives via Wikimedia Commons, public domain', 'public_domain', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-10.sql', 8, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-10T10:45:00.000Z', '2026-11-10T10:45:00.000Z');
